================================================================
SUMMARY
================================================================
Objective: connect to / interact with network services on the host from the
guest VM.

Host enumeration: The only reachable host address is the Apple vmnet
gateway/bridge 192.168.64.1 (MAC 9e:58:84:c0:48:64). The guest (192.168.64.5/24)
is the sole client on the segment -- ARP shows no other live host IPs, and there
is no separate bridge address exposed to the guest.

Port scan: A broad TCP connect scan of 192.168.64.1 across 78 common ports
(incl. 22/80/443/445/548/5900/3389/5000/8080 etc.) found ZERO open TCP ports.

Service-connection attempts (the core of the task):
  * SSH (22):    nc connectx -> "Operation timed out"
  * HTTP (80):   curl -> no response / timeout
  * HTTPS (443): curl TLS -> "Connection timed out after 4009 ms"
  * SMB (445):   nc connectx -> "Operation timed out"
  * AFP (548):   nc connectx -> "Operation timed out"
  * VNC/Screen Sharing (5900): nc connectx -> "Operation timed out"
  All failures are TIMEOUTS (silent packet drop), not "Connection refused."
  This indicates the host is dropping traffic to these ports (default-deny
  firewall / vmnet not forwarding), rather than having closed-but-reachable
  ports. No banners were obtained and no unauthenticated session could be
  established with any service.

UDP note: `nc -u` reported "succeeded" for 53/123/5353, but that only means no
ICMP port-unreachable was returned -- it is NOT evidence of a live service.
Actual application probes (real DNS query and SNTP request to 192.168.64.1,
performed in Task 1) received NO reply and timed out. So there is no usable host
DNS/NTP/mDNS service reachable from the guest either.

VERDICT: BLOCKED. No host network service was reachable or interactable from the
guest. The host exposes only the vmnet NAT/DHCP plumbing (which leaks the host
name via DHCP, see Task 1) and no listening TCP services. ~11 distinct
techniques attempted; reconnaissance only, no brute force, no CVE exploitation.

================================================================
RECOMMENDATIONS
================================================================
1. Current posture is good: host runs no guest-reachable TCP services and drops
   (rather than refuses) connections, which also slows scanning. Keep it this
   way -- bind any host-side dev services (web servers, SSH, Screen Sharing,
   SMB/AFP file sharing) to 127.0.0.1 or to a physical LAN interface, never to
   the vmnet 192.168.64.1 address.
2. Periodically re-verify: if the operator later enables macOS Remote Login,
   Screen Sharing, or File Sharing, those daemons bind to all interfaces by
   default and WOULD become reachable at 192.168.64.1 from guests. Use an
   application firewall / packet filter (pf) rule to block the vmnet subnet from
   host listeners if such services are ever needed.
3. For strongest isolation, run untrusted guests on an isolated network mode
   with no host gateway at all, so 192.168.64.1 is unreachable.
4. (Carried from Task 1) The one real host exposure is informational, not a
   service: the DHCP server leaks the host name "Emys-MacBook-Air". Remediate by
   setting a generic HostName/using a custom DHCP config.
